<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludvík Svoboda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludvík Svoboda was a Czechoslovak general who commanded the Czechoslovak units raised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1st-czechoslovak-army-corps">1st Czechoslovak Army Corps</a>. Defence minister after 1945, he was purged in the 1950s and later rehabilitated. As president from 1968 to 1975 he resisted Soviet pressure during the 1968 invasion but remained in office under normalisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludvik-svoboda.jpg" alt="Ludvík Svoboda" width="264" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original photo taken by Stanislav Tereba, The first derivated version by Fredy.00; This derivated version by Esc.eliska · CC BY-SA 3.0 cz · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludv%C3%ADk_Svoboda_1968.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludvík Svoboda was born on 25 November 1895 in Hroznatín, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. His father died when he was one year old. He attended the agricultural school at Velké Meziříčí and in 1915 had to join the Austro-Hungarian Army. He fell into Russian captivity at Tarnopol on 18 September 1915, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legion</a> and fought at Zborov and Bakhmach. In 1921 he began a career in the Czechoslovak Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the German occupation of 15 March 1939 Svoboda joined the underground organisation Obrana národa. In June 1939 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and, as the senior officer, formed a Czechoslovak unit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. After the defeat of Poland he led more than 700 officers and soldiers into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, where they were held in internment camps. For two years he negotiated with the Soviet authorities to keep the group together and send officers on to countries fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, he helped to prepare the agreement of 18 July 1941 that allowed a separate Czechoslovak unit. He then organised volunteers into a battalion that grew into a brigade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1st-czechoslovak-army-corps">1st Czechoslovak Army Corps</a>, the largest Czechoslovak unit abroad. In December 1943 he became a brigadier general, and on 10 September 1944 he took command of the corps.</p><p>He became defence minister in April 1945. During the crisis of February 1948 he remained in office when the other non-Communist ministers resigned and refused to use the army against the crowds, saying that the army would not march against the people. He then joined the Communist Party. Forced out of the army in 1950 under Soviet pressure, he was Deputy Prime Minister from 1950 to 1951, was imprisoned in the purges and was later released, and returned to public life at the wish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>.</p><p>Elected president on 30 March 1968 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s recommendation, he warned the Soviet ambassador against an invasion and, when it came in August, signed an order keeping the army out of it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>'s demand for a peasant-workers' government and secured the release of Dubček. He passively witnessed the removal of reformists and helped to replace Dubček with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> in April 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Husák never trusted him, and in 1975 an act of the Federal Assembly retired Svoboda, who had been in ill health, and Husák succeeded him. Svoboda died on 20 September 1979. He maintained to the end that his conduct before Brezhnev saved thousands of lives. Squares and streets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> still bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludvík Svoboda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Czechoslovak airmen flew with the Royal Air Force, a battalion fought at Tobruk, and the 1st Czechoslovak Army Corps under <b>Ludvík Svoboda</b> fought alongside the Red Army, including at the Dukla Pass in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Dubček replaced him as First Secretary on 5 January 1968, and on 22 March Novotný resigned as President and was replaced by <b>Ludvík Svoboda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hroznatín, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.3,15.91&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Hroznatín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 445 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludvík Svoboda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5367 715h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5367 715h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.3,15.91&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
